## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design modular prompt orchestration and a concrete lightweight prototype. Checks how candidates formalize routing signals and edge-case handling in real-world content workflows.

## Key Concepts

- Modular prompt architecture and routing signals
- Edge-case handling for content-critical prompts
- Lightweight Python prototype for quick validation
- Payload shaping for downstream components

## Code Example

```javascript
// Minimal routing prototype (JavaScript-like pseudocode)
function routePrompt(query) {
  const q = (query || '').toLowerCase();
  const module = (q.includes('source') || q.includes('earnings') || q.includes('did'))
    ? 'Source-Validator' 
    : q.includes('hallucination') ? 'Hallucination-Detector' : 'Answer-Formatter';
  return {
    module,
    plan: ['collect sources','assess credibility'],
    payload: { query, required_sources: 5, confidence_target: 'high' }
  };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test the routing decisions across diverse prompts?
- What metrics would you track to improve module selection over time?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design runtime policy enforcement and safe prompt routing under latency and drift.

## Key Concepts
- Runtime policy loading
- Injection detection and redaction
- Token budgeting and trimming
- Auditable decision logs

## Code Example
```javascript
// Minimal prototype sketch
const fetch = require('node-fetch');
async function buildPrompt(basePrompt){
  const policy = await fetch('http://localhost:8000/policy').then(r=>r.json());
  // injection guard
  let p = basePrompt.replace(/{{.*?}}/g, '');
  // secrets redaction
  p = p.replace(/(API_KEY|SECRET|PASSWORD)=[^\s]+/g, '[REDACTED]');
  // token cap (pseudo)
  const max = policy.maxTokens ?? 512; 
  return p.length > max ? p.slice(0, max) : p;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test resilience to policy drift over time?
- What instrumentation would you add to monitor latency and false positives in production?

## Why This Is Asked
Assesses ability to design a tiny, deterministic routing loop for an offline-first finance tool, with explicit module boundaries and safety signals.

## Key Concepts
- Prompt routing signals: intent, data_privacy, offline_capable
- Edge-case handling: no_cache, conflicting signals
- Module boundaries: TransactionFilter, SummaryComposer, LocalCacheManager
- Deterministic payload construction

## Code Example
```javascript
// Tiny router example (pseudo)
function routePrompt(prompt, offline) {
  const signals = extractSignals(prompt);
  const module = offline ? 'LocalCacheManager' : 'NetworkFetch';
  return {module, signals};
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test cache-miss scenarios and signal conflicts?
- How to extend to multi-account data and multiple currencies?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical prompt versioning and drift handling with concrete routing decisions for enterprise data assistants.

## Key Concepts
- Drift detection via keyword overlap scoring
- Baseline prompt management with patch generation
- Three-tier routing: Intent-Detector, Safety-Guard, Response-Composer
- Signal tracking: intent, data_sensitivity, version_delta
- Edge case handling: renamed intents, missing baselines

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-code for drift detection and routing
function routePrompt(newPrompt, baselinePrompt) {
  const overlap = calculateKeywordOverlap(newPrompt, baselinePrompt);
  const hasSafetyIssues = detectSafetyKeywords(newPrompt);

  if (hasSafetyIssues) return 'Safety-Guard';
  if (overlap < 0.6) return 'Intent-Detector';
  return 'Response-Composer';
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you calibrate the overlap threshold for different use cases?
- What approach would you use to store patches for audit trails?
- How would you handle version conflicts when multiple teams modify prompts simultaneously?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design scalable, privacy-aware prompt routing with safety checks under latency constraints, a common challenge in enterprise LLM deployments.

## Key Concepts

- Tenant-aware routing
- Privacy-preserving sanitization
- Latency-budget guided model selection
- Guardrails and veto logic
- Pluggable sanitizer architecture

## Code Example

```javascript
// Minimal gating prototype (JS)
class Gate {
  constructor() {}
  route(prompt, tenant) {
    const risk = {
      privacy: tenant.privacyRisk || 0,
      safety: tenant.safetyRisk || 0,
      latency: tenant.latencyBudget || 100
    };
    const model = risk.latency < 60 ? 'lite' : 'standard';
    const template = risk.privacy > 7 || risk.safety > 7 ? 'guarded' : 'default';
    return { tenant: tenant.id, model, template };
  }
}
const g = new Gate();
console.log(g.route('Prompt', {id: 'tenantA', privacyRisk:8, safetyRisk:5, latencyBudget:40}));
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test guardrails under adversarial prompts?
- How would you scale to thousands of tenants with dynamic policies?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design stable prompts across models and languages with guardrails.

## Key Concepts
- Prompt contracts, per-model templates, and intent extraction
- Drift testing and edge-case handling for ambiguity
- Guardrails and multilingual normalization
- Minimal prototype demonstration

## Code Example
```javascript
// Minimal prototype sketch
function routePrompt(userInput, context) {
  const templates = {
    en: { default: 'Template_EN' },
    fr: { default: 'Template_FR' }
  };
  const model = context.model || 'default';
  const lang = context.lang || 'en';
  const chosen = (templates[lang] && templates[lang].default) || templates['en'].default;
  const finalPrompt = chosen + '\n' + userInput;
  return { template: chosen, finalPrompt, guardrail: 'none' };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test cross-language behavior and detect prompt drift?
- How would you version templates and roll them out safely?
